<section class="section"><div class="wrap content">

<h2>The commercial formulas</h2><div class="formula-box">
<p><strong>NOI = Effective rental income − Operating expenses</strong>, where effective income is the gross rent after the vacancy allowance. <strong>DSCR = NOI ÷ Annual debt service</strong> — how many times the property's income covers its loan payments. <strong>Cap rate = NOI ÷ Property price</strong>. <strong>Cash-on-cash = Annual cash flow after debt service ÷ Total cash invested</strong> (down payment plus loan fees). The loan payment itself comes from the amortization period; the loan term only decides when the remaining balance — the balloon — falls due.</p>
</div>
<h2>Worked example</h2>
<p>With the values already filled in — a $750,000 property, 25% down and a $562,500 loan at 7.5% amortized over 25 years — the monthly payment is <strong>$4,156.83</strong>, or $49,881.90 a year. Gross rent of $96,000 with 8% vacancy gives effective income of $88,320; expenses of $9,000 tax, $4,500 insurance, $6,000 maintenance and a 5% management fee ($4,416) total $23,916, leaving <strong>NOI of $64,404</strong> and a <strong>cap rate of 8.59%</strong>. The DSCR is $64,404 ÷ $49,881.90 = <strong>1.29</strong>, cash flow is $14,522 a year ($1,210 a month), and the cash-on-cash return on the $193,125 invested is <strong>7.52%</strong>. After the 10-year term, a balloon of <strong>$448,411</strong> falls due.</p>
<h2>What is a good DSCR?</h2>
<p>DSCR is the lender's number first and the investor's second. At <strong>1.00</strong> the property exactly breaks even on its loan — one bad quarter puts it underwater. Most commercial lenders set their minimum between <strong>1.20 and 1.25</strong>, and will lend more, or price better, at <strong>1.35+</strong>. Between 1.00 and 1.20 a deal can still work for a buyer with reserves, but expect lenders to shrink the loan or raise the rate until the coverage fits their box. If your DSCR is short, the honest levers are a bigger down payment, a lower price, or verified higher rent — not optimistic expenses.</p>
<h2>The balloon payment: term vs amortization</h2>
<p>Commercial loans rarely run for their full amortization. A typical structure is a <strong>10-year term on a 25-year amortization</strong>: payments are calculated as if the loan had 25 years to run, but after year 10 the entire remaining balance — $448,411 in the example — is due at once. Almost nobody pays it from cash; the plan is to refinance or sell before it lands. That makes the balloon a date to manage, not a surprise to discover: know yours before you buy, and stress the deal against the interest rate you might refinance at, not today's.</p>
<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>DSCR is the gatekeeper: below about 1.20, most commercial lenders will not play, whatever the cap rate says.</li>
<li>Cap rate grades the property; cash-on-cash grades your money in it. A deal needs both.</li>
<li>The balloon is part of the price of the loan — a cheap payment with a balance you cannot refinance is not cheap.</li>
<li>Vacancy and management belong in every underwriting, even for a single-tenant building you plan to run yourself.</li>
</ul>
<p>Next steps: isolate the income figure in the <a href="../noi-calculator/">NOI Calculator</a>, value the same property at a target rate in the <a href="../cap-rate-calculator/">Cap Rate Calculator</a>, and compare a residential deal in the <a href="../rental-yield-calculator/">Rental Yield Calculator</a>.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li><strong>Underwriting with residential terms.</strong> A 30-year fixed fully-amortizing loan is a residential product. Commercial money is shorter, pricier and usually ends in a balloon — model what you will actually be offered.</li><li><strong>Quoting the cap rate as your return.</strong> The cap rate ignores the loan entirely. With leverage, your return is the cash-on-cash figure — and it can be higher or lower than the cap rate depending on the rate you borrow at.</li><li><strong>Using the seller's expense figures.</strong> Sellers' statements routinely omit management, understate maintenance and annualize a good quarter's rent. Rebuild expenses from tax records, quotes and your own management line.</li><li><strong>Forgetting the balloon in the exit plan.</strong> "Refinance later" is not a plan unless the property would still clear a 1.20 DSCR at a rate 2 points higher. Run that version before you commit.</li><li><strong>Assuming full occupancy.</strong> One empty unit in a four-unit building is a 25% vacancy event, not a rounding error. Small buildings need bigger vacancy allowances, not smaller ones.</li></ul><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<details class="faq"><summary>What does a commercial real estate calculator work out?</summary><p>It underwrites a commercial purchase: from the price, loan terms and rent roll it calculates net operating income (NOI), the cap rate, the debt service coverage ratio (DSCR), monthly and annual cash flow, cash-on-cash return, and the balloon balance due at the end of the loan term.</p></details>
<details class="faq"><summary>What DSCR do lenders require for a commercial loan?</summary><p>Most commercial lenders look for a DSCR of at least 1.20 to 1.25 — the property's NOI must be 20–25% higher than its annual loan payments. Stronger coverage (1.35+) usually means a larger loan or a better rate; below 1.20, expect the loan to shrink or the deal to be declined.</p></details>
<details class="faq"><summary>What is a balloon payment on a commercial loan?</summary><p>Commercial loans are often amortized over 20–25 years but mature in 5–10. When the term ends, the remaining balance — the balloon — is due in full. Borrowers normally refinance or sell before that date; this calculator shows the balloon amount so it is part of the plan from day one.</p></details>
<details class="faq"><summary>How much down payment does a commercial property need?</summary><p>Typically 20–35% of the price, depending on the property type, the borrower's strength and the DSCR at the requested loan size. Owner-occupied properties bought through SBA loans can need less. This calculator defaults to 25%, the middle of the usual range.</p></details>
<details class="faq"><summary>What is the difference between cap rate and cash-on-cash?</summary><p>The cap rate is NOI divided by the price — the property's unlevered return, the same for every buyer. Cash-on-cash is the annual cash flow after loan payments divided by the cash you actually invested, so it depends on your loan. The same building can show an 8.6% cap rate and a 7.5% cash-on-cash return.</p></details>
<details class="faq"><summary>Are commercial mortgage rates higher than residential?</summary><p>Usually yes — commercial loans carry more risk for the lender (income depends on tenants' businesses, terms are shorter, and the loans are rarely sold to government-backed buyers), so rates typically sit above residential mortgage rates, and 7.5% is a realistic planning figure rather than a quote.</p></details>

